 "t6_decontamination_ladder": {
  "added": "2026-08-17",
  "added_by": {
   "script": "/home/anont/.claude/jobs/5d56bbe0/tmp/t6_decontaminate.py",
   "sha256": "16a68f3fe67de21455eb62c3161922c50ec9b64d414acf06a5bdc641cbad1644"
  },
  "what": "The mean entry slippage at the 100 ms rung, in hundredths of a basis point of notional, with stock-days whose TRIGGER PRINT was more than `threshold_atr` ATR(14) through the stop removed. The cost model charges the MEAN, and the mean is the statistic a dislocated print moves most.",
  "why": "POSITIVE IS ADVERSE, so a print far through the stop produces a hugely FAVOURABLE measured gap and pulls the charged cost DOWN. Contamination here flatters the strategy, which makes it exactly the direction this programme is required to be paranoid about. The 1-ATR rung already in the file is too loose to remove the very print the result's own prose names (TSLA 2025-03-12, 0.92 ATR through).",
  "status": "POST HOC, and NOT a replacement for the preregistered distribution above. The preregistered result is the unfiltered one. This ladder exists so the cost model can charge a defensible rung and report the sensitivity next to it.",
  "unit": "hundredths of a basis point of the fill price (centi-bp), 100 ms latency rung",
  "recommended_rung": "0.1",
  "why_that_rung": "0.1 ATR is not an arbitrary cut: R -- the paper's own risk unit, the denominator of every verdict threshold in the preregistration -- is DEFINED as 10% of ATR(14). So the 0.1-ATR rung is 'the trigger print was more than one full R beyond the stop'. A print a whole risk-unit through the level is not that level being touched; it is a dislocated print. It is also the ADVERSE side of the ladder, which is the side this programme takes when it is uncertain.",
